<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#eid-al-ghadir">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eid al-Ghadir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Feast of Ghadir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eid al-Ghadir is a Shia Muslim festival held on 18 Dhu al-Hijja to commemorate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>'s sermon at Ghadir Khumm in 632, in which Shia Muslims see the designation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a> as his successor. The Buyid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> made it a public celebration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 963. It remains a major feast in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and among Shia communities elsewhere.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eid-al-ghadir.jpg" alt="Eid al-Ghadir" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amin Ahouei · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16-Ghadir_Eid_10_km_party-%D9%85%D9%87%D9%85%D9%88%D9%86%DB%8C_%DB%B1%DB%B0_%DA%A9%DB%8C%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85%D8%AA%D8%B1%DB%8C_%D8%B9%DB%8C%D8%AF_%D8%BA%D8%AF%DB%8C%D8%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
